WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.350
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:09.129
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.429 --> 00:00:18.014
Unit twenty-nine: Review: Evidence-Based Policy. Today you will argue about evidence using nominal clauses with the fact that.

4
00:00:18.314 --> 00:00:21.106
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:25.300
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:27.116
Evidence.

7
00:00:28.117 --> 00:00:31.405
Evidence means facts or data that support a conclusion.

8
00:00:32.017 --> 00:00:34.701
The evidence supported a change in policy.

9
00:00:35.317 --> 00:00:37.481
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

10
00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:41.439
Evidence.

11
00:00:42.183 --> 00:00:43.068
Policy.

12
00:00:44.083 --> 00:00:47.360
Policy means a course of action adopted by a government.

13
00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:50.228
Policy should respond to what works.

14
00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:53.515
Your turn. Say: Policy.

15
00:00:56.367 --> 00:00:57.298
Policy.

16
00:00:58.050 --> 00:00:59.272
Evaluation.

17
00:01:00.283 --> 00:01:04.094
Evaluation means a systematic assessment of whether something works.

18
00:01:04.683 --> 00:01:07.612
Evaluation showed the programme raised attendance.

19
00:01:08.217 --> 00:01:11.148
Your turn. Say: Evaluation.

20
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.164
Evaluation.

21
00:01:15.917 --> 00:01:16.674
Data.

22
00:01:17.683 --> 00:01:20.287
Data means recorded facts and figures.

23
00:01:20.883 --> 00:01:22.929
The data revealed a clear pattern.

24
00:01:23.517 --> 00:01:25.972
Your turn. Say: Data.

25
00:01:28.833 --> 00:01:29.753
Data.

26
00:01:30.517 --> 00:01:31.854
Trade-off.

27
00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:37.288
Trade-off means a balance in which gaining one thing means losing another.

28
00:01:37.883 --> 00:01:40.487
Every policy involves a trade-off.

29
00:01:41.083 --> 00:01:44.316
Your turn. Say: Trade-off.

30
00:01:47.167 --> 00:01:48.481
Trade-off.

31
00:01:49.217 --> 00:01:50.392
Stakeholders.

32
00:01:51.383 --> 00:01:54.881
Stakeholders means people or groups with an interest in a decision.

33
00:01:55.483 --> 00:01:57.936
Stakeholders must be consulted early.

34
00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:01.226
Your turn. Say: Stakeholders.

35
00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:05.137
Stakeholders.

36
00:02:05.883 --> 00:02:07.175
Implementation.

37
00:02:08.183 --> 00:02:11.750
Implementation means the process of putting a plan into practice.

38
00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:15.069
Weak implementation undermined a good design.

39
00:02:15.683 --> 00:02:18.475
Your turn. Say: Implementation.

40
00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:22.752
Implementation.

41
00:02:23.517 --> 00:02:24.460
Impact.

42
00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:28.378
Impact means the effect of an action or policy.

43
00:02:28.983 --> 00:02:32.214
The impact of the programme was measured after two years.

44
00:02:32.817 --> 00:02:35.214
Your turn. Say: Impact.

45
00:02:38.067 --> 00:02:38.905
Impact.

46
00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:43.409
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:49.062
Visal, the study proved the programme worked, yet the ministry cancelled it. Why?

48
00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:58.561
The fact that evidence exists does not guarantee its use. Politics, cost and opinion all intervene.

49
00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:01.452
But isn't evidence decisive?

50
00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:12.157
Not alone. The fact that a policy works does not mean that stakeholders will support it, and the fact that data are imperfect gives critics an excuse.

51
00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:15.191
So what should researchers do?

52
00:03:16.283 --> 00:03:26.192
Present findings clearly and acknowledge trade-offs. The fact that implementation matters as much as design should shape every evaluation.

53
00:03:27.333 --> 00:03:29.031
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:29.883 --> 00:03:33.276
Grammar focus: Nominal clauses with the fact that.

55
00:03:33.576 --> 00:03:34.763
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:38.773
The fact that evidence exists does not guarantee its use.

57
00:03:39.683 --> 00:03:43.204
The fact that a policy works does not ensure public support.

58
00:03:44.117 --> 00:03:47.962
The fact that implementation matters should shape every evaluation.

59
00:03:48.900 --> 00:04:00.251
The fact that + full clause acts as a noun phrase: subject or object. It treats the clause as a settled fact. The verb after it agrees with 'fact' (singular): The fact that … does not guarantee.

60
00:04:01.017 --> 00:04:05.085
Be careful! Don't say: The fact evidence exists is enough.

61
00:04:07.033 --> 00:04:10.612
Say: The fact that evidence exists is not enough.

62
00:04:10.912 --> 00:04:12.598
the fact that + clause.

63
00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:16.052
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:16.867 --> 00:04:19.064
When Evidence Meets Politics.

65
00:04:19.364 --> 00:04:24.637
Evidence-based policy asks that decisions be guided by rigorous evaluation of what works.

66
00:04:25.633 --> 00:04:31.325
Randomised trials and careful data analysis can reveal the impact of a programme with surprising clarity.

67
00:04:32.333 --> 00:04:39.720
Yet the fact that evidence exists does not guarantee its use, because policymakers weigh cost, public opinion and competing interests.

68
00:04:40.733 --> 00:04:46.088
The fact that most policies involve trade-offs means that evidence informs choices rather than making them.

69
00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:53.082
Good implementation and honest communication with stakeholders are therefore as important as the original analysis.

70
00:04:54.167 --> 00:04:58.145
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:58.983 --> 00:05:02.666
The fact blank evidence exists does not guarantee its use.

72
00:05:02.966 --> 00:05:05.714
A, that. B, which. C, what.

73
00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:10.860
The answer is A: that.

74
00:05:12.017 --> 00:05:15.351
A systematic assessment of whether something works is an blank.

75
00:05:15.651 --> 00:05:20.106
A, evaluation. B, impact only. C, implementation.

76
00:05:23.667 --> 00:05:25.852
The answer is A: evaluation.

77
00:05:27.017 --> 00:05:30.874
A balance in which gaining one thing means losing another is a blank.

78
00:05:31.174 --> 00:05:35.582
A, data. B, trade-off. C, policy.

79
00:05:39.133 --> 00:05:42.073
The answer is B: trade-off.

80
00:05:43.217 --> 00:05:45.019
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:45.319 --> 00:05:52.850
A, The fact that it works is encouraging.. B, The fact it that works is encouraging.. C, The fact that it work are encouraging..

82
00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:59.630
The answer is A: The fact that it works is encouraging..

83
00:06:00.783 --> 00:06:04.188
According to the reading, evidence informs choices rather than…

84
00:06:04.488 --> 00:06:08.083
A, making them. B, existing. C, improving.

85
00:06:11.633 --> 00:06:13.610
The answer is A: making them.

86
00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:18.207
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:22.083
Why is evidence sometimes ignored in policymaking?

88
00:06:38.883 --> 00:06:40.407
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:40.707 --> 00:07:07.803
Evidence is sometimes ignored because decisions involve more than facts: cost, public opinion, timing and the interests of powerful groups all matter. The fact that a policy works does not mean that voters will like it. Evidence can also be uncertain or arrive too late. Researchers can help by presenting findings clearly and acknowledging trade-offs.

90
00:07:08.103 --> 00:07:09.743
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:15.392
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and nominal clauses with the fact that.

92
00:07:15.692 --> 00:07:21.212
Today's value is intellectual honesty. Follow the evidence, even when it is inconvenient.

93
00:07:21.512 --> 00:07:28.242
Congratulations! You have finished Online C two, Book three. See you in Online C two, Book four!

94
00:07:28.542 --> 00:07:31.554
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

